Megatron is a damage cannon that fires artifacts. On the Vehicle side, every attack sacrifices an artifact and deals damage equal to its MANA VALUE to a creature — and any excess goes to that creature's controller. Sacrifice an eleven-drop, point it at a 1/1, and eleven damage hits a face. The deck is built to have enormous artifacts in the graveyard, get them onto the battlefield cheaply, and feed them to the cannon.

# The commander
Megatron, Tyrant :: cast him CONVERTED for {1}{R}{W}{B} — four mana instead of his printed cost — and he arrives as the Vehicle with living metal, so he is a creature on your turn without needing crew.
On the Tyrant side, opponents cannot cast spells during combat at all, and converting in your postcombat main adds {C} for every point of life your opponents lost this turn. A big cannon shot pays for the next fatty.
Basilisk Collar :: this is the multiplier. Deathtouch means one damage is lethal to ANY creature, so every point past the first is excess and goes to the face. Sacrificing a mana value twelve artifact deals eleven to a player regardless of what you pointed it at.
Isshin, Two Heavens as One :: Megatron's attack trigger is an attack trigger, so it happens twice. Two artifacts sacrificed, two bursts of damage, in one combat.
Lightning Greaves :: haste and shroud.

# The ammunition
Every one of these is a huge mana value, which is the only stat the cannon reads.
Excalibur, Sword of Eden :: mana value twelve, and its cost falls by your historic permanents, so it is castable as well as loadable.
Metalwork Colossus :: mana value eleven, nearly free to cast with noncreature artifacts out, and it returns itself from the graveyard for two artifacts.
Mycosynth Golem :: mana value eleven, and it gives every artifact creature spell affinity so the rest of the ammunition costs almost nothing.
Panther Robot :: mana value ten with affinity.
Ancient Stone Idol :: mana value ten, flash, and it leaves a 6/12 behind when it dies.
Phyrexian Triniform :: mana value nine, and it dies into three 3/3s.
Bygone Colossus :: mana value nine, and warp lets you play it for three now and cast it properly later.
Cityscape Leveler :: mana value eight, destroys a permanent on cast and on every attack, and unearths.
Imskir Iron-Eater :: a second cannon — sacrifice an artifact to deal its mana value to ANY target, no creature required.

# Getting them into the graveyard and back
Buried Alive :: three fatties straight into the yard.
Faithless Looting :: cheap filtering, twice.
Goblin Engineer :: tutors an artifact directly into the graveyard, then recurs cheap ones.
Goblin Welder :: swaps an artifact you control for a better one in the graveyard, every turn, free.
Mishra, Tamer of Mak Fawa :: every artifact card in your graveyard gains unearth {1}{B}{R}. That is three mana to put a mana-value-eleven cannon shell onto the battlefield, and it also gives your whole board a ward.
Daretti, Rocketeer Engineer :: returns an artifact from the graveyard on entry and on every attack.
Daretti, Scrap Savant :: rummages, reanimates an artifact on the minus, and the ultimate returns every artifact that dies from then on.
Kuldotha Forgemaster :: three artifacts becomes any artifact in the deck, straight onto the battlefield.
Animate Dead :: cheap reanimation for the artifact creatures.
Necromancy :: at flash speed, so you can rebuild in response to a wrath.
Persist :: two mana for any nonlegendary creature.
Morbid Curiosity :: sacrifice a fatty and draw cards equal to its mana value. Eleven cards is not unusual.

# Making the attack happen
Krang, Utrom Warlord :: every other artifact creature gains flying, trample, indestructible and haste. It turns a board of ammunition into an army.
Anger :: sitting in the graveyard with a Mountain out, everything you reanimate has haste.
Grand Abolisher :: nobody interacts on your turn.
Megatron, Tyrant :: the Tyrant side already stops opponents casting during combat, which covers the window that matters most.
Boros Charm :: indestructible for the board through a wrath, or double strike, or four to the face.
Clever Concealment :: usually free with convoke, and it phases the board out of a sweeper.
Deflecting Swat :: free, and it redirects the removal aimed at Megatron mid-combat.

# Removal
Demonic Junker :: destroys a creature from every player at once, and grows if one of yours died.
Organic Extinction :: improvise makes it cheap, and it destroys every NONARTIFACT creature — a one-sided wrath here.
Cityscape Leveler :: a permanent destroyed on cast and on every attack.
Combustible Gearhulk :: three cards, or three milled cards' worth of damage — and milling yourself loads the cannon.
Imskir Iron-Eater :: draws off your artifact count and throws artifacts at anything.

# Mana and tutors
Mox Opal :: metalcraft is trivial here.
Arcane Signet :: fixing three colours.
Fellwar Stone :: usually taps for what you need.
Mycosynth Golem :: affinity on every artifact creature spell — the single biggest cost reducer.
Metalwork Colossus :: effectively free with a few rocks down.
Demonic Tutor :: any card.
Enlightened Tutor :: any artifact, to the top.
Esper Sentinel :: taxes their first noncreature spell each turn.

# What to keep in an opening hand
Keep a graveyard enabler and acceleration. Buried Alive or Goblin Engineer plus a rock means Megatron converts on turn four with a mana-value-eleven shell already waiting in the yard.
Cast Megatron CONVERTED. Four mana for the Vehicle side is the whole point; the Tyrant side is where you flip TO after the damage, not where you start.
Basilisk Collar :: find it before you find more ammunition. Without deathtouch you need a small creature to point at; with it, every shot dumps almost its full mana value into a player.
Convert in your postcombat main phase. The mana it adds scales with the life your opponents lost that turn, so a big cannon shot funds a second fatty the same turn.
